11223	{"artifactId":"LLD-315d510cb28100e2-s8","entry":{"id":"review-313fc6f7224e0be550da2fd9993c2443","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-22T17:26:51.191Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"ac38 (someone who does not hold the claim is refused when revising an artifact in an open request) is fully covered by asking the unchanged teamGate with 'revise' from assertMayAuthor, for every stage in signoff including the DEF (define: DEF).","evidence":"Two gaps. (1) gate.ts:280 `if (holder === null && !CLAIMING.has(action)) return allowed();` with CLAIMING = ['author','build'] (gate.ts:139). So 'revise' on an unassigned unit is ALLOWED. The design keeps teamGate unchanged (2.4), so an unassigned user would be let through, not refused. (2) author-gate.ts:25-35 authorUnitOf returns undefined for 'define' (only design.epic, design.story and plan get a unit), and assertMayAuthor returns at line 40. So a DEF revision is never gated, even though §2.3 says 'define: DEF'. [files: src/workflow/team/author-gate.ts, src/workflow/team/gate.ts]","action":"Say how the unassigned case is refused. Either assertMayAuthor refuses on its own when the 'revise' gate returns allowed with no holder (it would read the assignee itself), or an amendment to sc4 (owned by s5) makes 'revise' refuse when nobody holds the unit. Also either extend authorUnitOf to define runs on an existing epicHash, or drop DEF from the claim and explain why.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"ac36 and ac37 (local approval again before re-push; single thread; no carry-over of an earlier completion) are designed through openSignoff resubmission to the same request.","evidence":"DEF.md:242-243 match the design's aims. lifecycle.ts:163-168: a content change returns the head to draft at a new revision. lifecycle.ts:250-253: open-signoff appends a new entry at the current revision. A completion therefore cannot cover a later revision, and §2.1 reuses the request named by the earlier entry.","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"signoffViewOf returns 'the latest Signoff entry of the head', and openSignoff can use it to find the earlier request of a revised head.","evidence":"signoff.ts:141-148 keeps only entries with `s.revision === current`. After a content change the head is at revision n+1, and its earlier entry is at revision n. signoffViewOf then returns NO_SIGNOFF for the revised, re-approved head, so a resubmission built on it would always open a new request. [files: src/workflow/team/signoff.ts]","action":"State that the resubmission looks up the last entry of signoffsOfMeta(meta) whatever its revision, not signoffViewOf, and fix the §2.5 description.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"prerequisiteFailure already refuses an artifact 'without an other-party review of the current revision' (error path 1).","evidence":"signoff.ts:415-418 checks only that lifecycleReviewOf(meta) exists and was run by the other party; it never compares the review's revision with the current one. The current-revision review is enforced only by 'approve' (lifecycle.ts:233-236), and an override reason skips that check. So an override-approved revision with a stale review passes. [files: src/workflow/team/signoff.ts, src/workflow/artifact-core/lifecycle.ts]","action":"Either add a check that the review's revision equals the current revision to prerequisiteFailure as part of this Story, or reword the detection to say it relies on the approve step.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"After a revision is pushed to the same pull request, settle records signed-off for the new revision when the request completes (the readSignoffState mapping supports the revise loop).","evidence":"github-adapter.ts:190 `if (parsed.state === 'CLOSED' || parsed.reviewDecision === 'CHANGES_REQUESTED') return 'changes-requested';`. On GitHub the CHANGES_REQUESTED review decision stays after a new push until the reviewer re-reviews or dismisses it. The first settle after a resubmission therefore records signoff-changes on the new revision at once. waitingHeads (signoff.ts:540) then skips the head because it is no longer in-signoff, and a later merge is never recorded. The design does not mention this. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github-adapter.ts, src/workflow/team/signoff.ts]","action":"Design how a stale CHANGES_REQUESTED decision is told apart from one on the new push. For example, compare the latest review's commit or submittedAt with the push or the entry's `at`, or treat changes-requested as current only when a review newer than the entry's open time asks for it. Add a contract test and an integration test for it.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"readSignoffRequest is new, and the existing port has no read of a request's head branch or openness. openSignoffRequest already reuses an open pull request on the branch (the fallback when the port has no readSignoffRequest).","evidence":"port.ts:112-113 has only openSignoffRequest and readSignoffState (no branch). github-adapter.ts:158-160 reuses `openPullRequestOf(owner, repo, spec.branch)` before `gh pr create`.","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"'revise' is already a guarded and hooked action that never takes a claim, so no gate type change is needed.","evidence":"gate.ts:30 GuardedAction includes 'revise'; gate.ts:121 HOOKED and gate.ts:136 GUARDED include it; gate.ts:139 CLAIMING is author and build only. signoffCompletionHook (signoff.ts:311-313) continues for 'revise'.","action":"None. The unassigned consequence is reported under coverage-of-intent.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"assertMayAuthor is the single place to switch 'author' to 'revise', and switching does not lose the signoff block of the followed stage.","evidence":"assertMayAuthor is called at start.ts:62, plan.ts:40, step.ts:38, synthesize.ts:40/70 and workflow-rpc.ts:184/343/485, all through one function. The followed-stage signoff block is enforced separately by settleBeforeAuthor → settleForAttempt (signoff.ts:639-645), which still passes 'author'. Skipping the hook for 'revise' therefore does not bypass it.","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The branch check can compare the branch publishBranch returns with readSignoffRequest's branch, and nothing is recorded on a mismatch.","evidence":"publishArtifactsBranch (signoff.ts:370-385) commits, pushes and returns the current branch name, and recording happens only afterwards in recordOpened (signoff.ts:515-525). Note that the push to the current (wrong) branch has already happened by the time of the comparison. The design says only 'nothing is recorded', which is accurate.","action":"Optionally, compare the branch before publishing so nothing is pushed to the wrong branch.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No stored shape changes: older Signoff entries stay readable, and several entries naming one request are allowed by the existing model.","evidence":"signoffsOfMeta (signoff.ts:119-131) reads revision, request, state and at per entry with no uniqueness constraint. open-signoff appends (lifecycle.ts:253). signoffRecordsFromHeads dedupes per (artifactId, revision) within a request group (signoff.ts:180).","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design changes no lifecycle table entry (owned by sc3/s4) and no gate outcome or hook type (sc4/s5); it only adds an optional port read and a gate call.","evidence":"The chosen alternative a1 adds no lifecycle operation. LIFECYCLE_TABLE (lifecycle.ts:38-52) already moves a content change back to draft through reconcileLifecycle. The TrackerPort addition is optional. The unassigned-refusal gap reported above would need either an sc4 change or local handling in assertMayAuthor, and that must stay within this boundary.","action":"None, beyond resolving the coverage finding without editing gate.ts silently.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A read failure of the earlier request is returned rather than falling back to a new request, and a missing readSignoffRequest falls back safely to s7 behaviour.","evidence":"PortResult failure kinds come from attempt() in the adapter, so a failed read can be detected and returned. Without the method, openRequest → openSignoffRequest reuses an open pull request on the branch (github-adapter.ts:158-160), so no duplicate pull request is opened when the branch matches.","action":"None. The stale CHANGES_REQUESTED decision after a push is reported under current-behaviour.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each of ac36, ac37 and ac38 maps to at least one test, at unit or contract level and at integration level.","evidence":"The acceptance mapping table in §6 names unit and integration tests for ac36, ac37 and ac38, plus a contract test for readSignoffRequest. The ac38 unit test expects 'an unassigned user with a claim-first refusal', which will fail against the unchanged gate (gate.ts:280), so the test exposes the design gap rather than missing it.","action":"Add a test in which a reviewer's earlier CHANGES_REQUESTED decision survives the re-push, and a test for revising a DEF through define.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":2,"med":2,"low":9,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-22T17:26:51.191Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
